WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.876 --> 00:00:03.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.890
CRAIG BOX: Good
morning, everybody.

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.980
Thank you for coming
along to a talk today.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.950
Want to tell you
about how we improve

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:16.120
utilization and portability of
applications using containers.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:16.930
My name is Craig.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.060
I'm an advocate for the
Google Cloud platform,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.080
focused on IT professionals,
and also dev ops.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.520
It's great to be here on a
fantastic warm, sunny day

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.320
in San Francisco.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.516
But because I've come over
from London with my girlfriend,

00:00:31.516 --> 00:00:33.390
we're going to spend
the weekend in Yosemite.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.700
And we thought we needed to pack
our snow boots just in case.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.820
Her boots didn't
fit in her suitcase.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.860
But I had lots of
empty space in mine.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.020
So we put her boots in
the bag, and put them

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.580
inside my suitcase.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.942
So I've increased the
utilization of my suitcase.

00:00:46.942 --> 00:00:48.900
And by doing that, that
means we don't actually

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.790
have to pay to take
another bag with us.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.040
You can tell I'm probably
not hipster enough for those

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.170
are my actual suitcases.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.389
But also in saying that,
I'm not the kind of person

00:00:57.389 --> 00:00:58.930
who's going to vacuum
pack my shirts,

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.980
so that I can fit
more in my suitcase.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.489
But if you are the airport, and
you're moving thousands of bags

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.030
around every minute, being able
to pack more into your trucks

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.671
does actually save
you a lot of money.

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:14.420
We, of course, have a
very similar problem

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.610
with the computers
here at Google.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.940
Every new computer
we add costs money.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.550
But we have more computers
than most people do.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.820
So we want to pack more
things onto fewer machines

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.720
and improve the
utilization of them.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.070
So by building a system that
allows computers to decide how

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.260
to do the packing,
rather than people,

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.370
we found that, for example, we
could run our batch workloads,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.295
like building the search
index for Google.com,

00:01:41.295 --> 00:01:44.800
into the empty space on
the same set of machines

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.450
that run our serving workloads,
like serving you 10 blue links

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.490
when you go to do a search.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.110
And we've estimated
that at the time--

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.360
and this was a few years ago--

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.250
just that one thing, being
able to pack the batch

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.410
into the empty space saved
us the cost of building

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.470
an entire Google data center.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:03.720
These are fantastic buildings.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.960
But as you can imagine,
they don't come cheap.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.639
How do we do this?

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:12.480
We need to move away from
managing individual machines

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.330
to managing fleets
of applications

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.820
running on containers.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.295
So first of all, a quick
refresh on virtual machines

00:02:22.295 --> 00:02:24.670
and containers because a lot
of the workloads that people

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.450
want to move to cloud today and
into containers start on VMs.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.600
So we want to look at a recap
of the strengths and weaknesses

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.367
of the two platforms.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.540
Ultimately, I like to
think of a container

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.440
as just being a process with
some restrictions on what it

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.670
can and can't do.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.350
If you think of an
app on your phone,

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.310
it's a very close analogy
to a Linux container.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.930
If you turn on your phone, you
start the operating system.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.490
It's booting a machine.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.000
It takes around about 60 seconds
from cold to get to the point

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.400
where you can use, any computer
really, but phones even so.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.880
Starting a process is just
launching an application.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.390
If you don't have it yet,
then you have to download it.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:09.644
It takes a few seconds.

00:03:09.644 --> 00:03:11.060
But when you do
have it, it should

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.830
take milliseconds to launch.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:17.380
It should be imperceptible
in the most case.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.900
Now for a lot of reasons, the
applications on your phone

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.842
are only allowed to
do a subset of things.

00:03:22.842 --> 00:03:24.550
You want to restrict
an app, for example,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.890
not to be able to download
your entire contact list

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.780
and send it out, and
[? use ?] something.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.200
The permissions you
set are about what

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.620
you want to grant a
particular application to do.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.710
And similarly, Linux containers
have restrictions set on them

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.020
to what they can and
can't do, especially

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.050
with regard to the resources
that they can see and use

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.937
on the machines that
they're running on.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.020
Now some of the characteristics
of virtual machines

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.020
cause us to have
lower utilization.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.120
For example, in this case, we
have a host operating system.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.350
But in our three VMs, we
run three redundant copies

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.950
of a guest OS.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.970
In that image, we
coupled in the libraries

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.100
that relate to the
particular operating system

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.042
that we're running.

00:04:08.042 --> 00:04:09.500
And therefore, the
apps that we run

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.470
use those system libraries
in a tight coupling.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.529
And even though we
have some free space

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.170
available on all three of
the virtual machines running

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:22.980
on this particular host, if we
want to run something that is,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.560
say, three units
big, in this case,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.524
we can't do that because the
space is stranded across three

00:04:28.524 --> 00:04:29.315
different machines.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.770
Now containers abstract away the
operating system, first of all,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.550
so that you, as a developer,
all you have to think about

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:44.910
is your own application and the
libraries that it needs to run.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.037
You think, also,
about the interfaces

00:04:47.037 --> 00:04:48.870
with the other applications
you want to run.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.010
And if we only had one machine
that never failed and never ran

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.480
out of resources, that
would be all that we needed.

00:05:01.404 --> 00:05:03.070
Because we run on
more than one machine,

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.840
and we want to be able
to scale horizontally,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.790
we invented Kubernetes--

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.430
which is derivative of
our internal systems,

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.650
but developed as open source--

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.510
to abstract away
everything else that you

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.300
might need to think about in
a multi-machine environment,

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.080
which we call a cluster.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.084
So scheduling
containers is one part.

00:05:21.084 --> 00:05:23.000
But other things, like
keeping your containers

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.090
alive and healthy, grouping
them together into the services

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.400
that you actually want to
run, connecting traffic

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.760
to those services, handling,
logging, and monitoring,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.680
and identify and
authorization, these

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.110
are all things that we feel,
as an application developer,

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:37.840
the system should
provide for you.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:43.980
Now those things
make up two legs

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.050
of what we've come
to call cloud native.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.600
So container packaging
gives you predictability

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.410
of deployment and efficient
resource isolation.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.690
So you're just
copying a file when

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.490
you're deploying
a container that

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.210
contains all of the
resources, libraries

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.390
that your application needs.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.610
Next is the dynamic
scheduling-- which is, again,

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.350
provided by Kubernetes--

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.200
that allows higher quality
of service and efficiency

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.000
by being able to have
the computer decide

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.860
where to place these workloads.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.830
And that means that you can
do more with fewer humans

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.790
and have a lower
operations cost.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.350
Now the final leg
of cloud native

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.510
is microservices oriented.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.880
And that talks more to
the re-usability of code,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.630
and independently developed
applications, and sort

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:32.180
of domain-driven
development as much

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.070
as it talks of loose coupling.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.140
It's not necessarily
something that everyone

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.090
will do as they move
their apps to the cloud.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.200
But it's something
to think about

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.550
for your newer applications.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:47.930
So I want to talk
through a few use cases

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:52.730
from both a business and
a technical perspective.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:55.460
For example, what do you
think the most common cause

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.200
of outages is?

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.170
Do you think it's people
writing bad software?

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.450
It turns out that it's
actually people deploying

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:04.340
misconfigured software.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.961
And it's especially
the case when

00:07:05.961 --> 00:07:08.210
you have the kind of bad
outages that bring everything

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:08.900
down at once.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.760
We find that we're
seeing studies

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:16.421
with over 50% of outages caused
by bad configuration pushes.

00:07:16.421 --> 00:07:18.170
Another point, our
friends at Ticketmaster

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:21.530
have estimated that simply
the action of moving code

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:24.260
around and deploying it
into their environments

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.770
was a $60 to $90 million
a year problem for them,

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.810
the amount of
resource and people

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.270
and machinery required
to deploy code

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:36.454
across hundreds of different
bespoke systems that they run.

00:07:36.454 --> 00:07:38.870
And ultimately, this leads to
questions like, why can't we

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:39.680
move quickly?

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:41.180
Why are these
deployments failing?

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:44.750
And why are we having to run
servers at 20% utilization

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:47.600
when we're wasting and
stranding all this excess space?

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.120
We need to know that our
deployments will succeed.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.172
So I want to talk about how we
can solve some of these things

00:07:55.172 --> 00:07:56.380
with a cloud native approach.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.800
Now I think there are
three distinct changes

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.110
that you need to think about
if you're going to this move.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.120
Just as cloud native
has three legs,

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.830
we have these three
distinct migrations

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.250
with individual characteristics.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.780
So you can move to containers
without thinking about any

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.100
of the dynamic scheduling.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.130
You can just deploy a single
container on a single machine

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.820
and treat them like an RPM or
a package of some description.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.310
That's a perfectly
valid thing to do.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:30.260
The other end, you could choose
to run everything in VMs.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.510
You can run clustered
applications.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:35.270
You don't actually
need to worry about--

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:37.490
we think a container's a
great format for clustering.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:41.000
But there are other
approaches that you can run.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.850
Now cloud native doesn't
actually require cloud.

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:45.440
You can take those
first two things

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.930
and run in your own environment,
move your application

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.739
to container clusters,
and run it on premise.

00:08:50.739 --> 00:08:52.280
And we think that's
a fantastic thing

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.820
to do because when it comes
time to move to cloud,

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.240
you'll find that all the hard
work has been done for you.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.960
Makes your application portable.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:04.570
And we want to talk about
some specific workloads

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:05.820
and what that might look like.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.940
First of all, a
three-tier application,

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:14.210
we quite often think of a
presentation, or web front end,

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.390
some sort of middleware,
and some sort of database.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:21.380
In this particular environment,
we've already made separation.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:25.100
We have a distinct, clear
contract between these layers.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:29.460
And it's quite often a network
communication as it stands.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.894
So it's easy to scale
them out independently.

00:09:31.894 --> 00:09:33.560
And so if you have a
workload like this,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.643
it's a really good candidate
to be the first thing

00:09:35.643 --> 00:09:38.094
that you move, for the
first two tiers, at least.

00:09:38.094 --> 00:09:40.010
We're going to talk about
databases separately

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.090
in a minute.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:45.590
The batch jobs are another
great use case because

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:47.450
of their burstable nature.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:50.690
If you can pack your batch
work into containers,

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.522
then you can schedule
them only when

00:09:52.522 --> 00:09:54.980
your cluster has free space,
as we talked about with Google

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:56.960
Search earlier.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:59.250
There are package
management options now

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:03.040
for running things like
Spark on top of Kubernetes.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.540
And if you want to run
clustered batch processing

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.880
stuff on Google, you
also have hosted options

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.270
like Cloud Dataflow
and Cloud Dataproc.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.740
And both of those
things will let

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:17.400
you take a Docker
container, and use it

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.190
as the unit of processing
that you do on your data.

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:23.450
So again, these two things
can be decoupled and used

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:24.380
separately.

00:10:26.914 --> 00:10:28.580
Now a lot of people
talk about something

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:31.400
which we call carving the
monolith, which is an industry

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.840
term for taking a
single, giant application

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.320
and breaking it up
into microservices.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.770
For more traditional
workloads, I

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.940
like to think you can apply the
same logic to virtual machines.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.630
Kubernetes has a
concept of a pod, which

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:46.587
is the unit of scheduling.

00:10:46.587 --> 00:10:48.170
It's not just an
individual container,

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:52.510
but it's containers that
tightly couple together

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.260
that need to be scheduled
at the same time

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.210
and need to communicate
with each other.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.536
And this lets you break down
your work into containers

00:10:59.536 --> 00:11:00.410
where it makes sense.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.576
And you don't need to pack
two different things that

00:11:02.576 --> 00:11:05.210
are sort of built by different
teams into the same container,

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:08.690
have them maintain them,
connect them together in a pod,

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:11.300
and then you can deploy them
on different life cycles.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.670
And eventually,
that just moves you

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:16.130
towards a microservices
implementation.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:19.070
But if you have VMs today where
you use something like Puppet,

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.260
for example, to deploy
a bunch of things,

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.010
you think about how
we can break them down

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.700
to individual containers
and then map them to a pod.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:32.174
Now databases are a
very contentious topic.

00:11:32.174 --> 00:11:34.340
There's a popular meme
saying you probably shouldn't

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:38.070
run databases in containers.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.400
But remember that
all the container is

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.570
is a Linux process
with some restrictions

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.719
about what it can and can't do.

00:11:44.719 --> 00:11:47.010
So again, think about the
difference between containers

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:48.870
and clusters.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:52.160
It is possible to run
Oracle on top of Docker.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:55.230
Oracle even published
images on GitHub

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.050
that let you build
containers to run this.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:00.270
It's possible that you
don't want these dynamically

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:02.150
scheduled and just moved
around your environment

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.320
at will because you actually
need to connect them up

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.570
to the data that backs them.

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:09.060
But there are more modern,
more cloud native databases

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:13.020
that support this kind
of clustering themselves.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.850
So the way I look about
this, it pays to ask,

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:18.700
do I get value out
of being a DBA?

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:19.700
Think about it this way.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.510
If you know how to scale the
database, how to back it up,

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:24.220
how it deals in
cluster partitions,

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:27.520
how it deals in
network failures,

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:30.250
and you know how to do all the
same thing with a cluster, yes,

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:33.070
it is absolutely possible to
take a traditional database--

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:35.000
something that's
not cluster aware--

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.440
and run it on top of Kubernetes.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.390
If you're dealing with
a commercial vendor,

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.040
they might not like
you doing this.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.870
They might say, well, that's
not a supported environment.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.203
I'm not really happy about that.

00:12:44.203 --> 00:12:45.480
We'd rather you run on VMs.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:46.310
And that's OK.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:49.200
Because you can just run
those VMs next to your cluster

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.440
and call out to them.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.870
With open source databases,
you sort of weigh up the pros

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:58.010
and cons of hosted services--
and you've all seen that we

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.680
announced our hosted PostgreSQL
service in the keynote this

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:01.500
morning--

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:02.834
versus running your own.

00:13:02.834 --> 00:13:04.250
And with our hosted
service, there

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.290
are some extensions that
we just don't provide,

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:07.850
that you may need to run custom.

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:09.680
And there may well
be specific-use cases

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:11.390
where it does make
sense to run your own.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:16.330
And my colleagues from Samsung
will talk about that in a bit.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:20.040
Patterns and helpers for running
these databases on Kubernetes

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.726
are coming thick and
fast from the community.

00:13:22.726 --> 00:13:24.850
Vitess, what you see there
on the logo on the left,

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.340
is a new application
developed by YouTube

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.910
which makes it possible to
scale MySQL horizontally.

00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:32.860
It works well on Kubernetes.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:36.430
It a great way of running
MySQL in a way that

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.290
is aware of the
clustered environment

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:39.790
where workloads can
move and change.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.040
But remember, it's
not all or nothing.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.560
It's still OK to use VMs.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:49.200
You can use the service
abstraction in Kubernetes

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:53.460
to refer to things outside your
cluster, as well as inside it.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:55.715
If you do have a workload
where locality is important,

00:13:55.715 --> 00:13:58.890
where you want to make sure
it's tied to a physical machine

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:02.310
that has a card, or a GPU,
or a license and dongle,

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:03.360
for example.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.490
Or if you already run
something that's commercial

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.380
and has cluster
manager in its name,

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.810
chances are you'll want to
keep those things separate.

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:12.926
And because we have both
container and VM platforms,

00:14:12.926 --> 00:14:14.175
it's easy to make that choice.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:20.000
OK, we've made some choices
about what applications

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.366
we want to move.

00:14:21.366 --> 00:14:22.990
The first step is
actually putting them

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:26.140
inside a container.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:28.781
So for applications that you
build yourself internally,

00:14:28.781 --> 00:14:30.280
the easiest thing
to do is just take

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.040
the output from your
current build system--

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.960
in this case, we have an RPM--

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:38.590
and then write a little
script or Dockerfile

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:41.170
to build a container
based on installing

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:44.276
that on top of an image
that already has legs on it.

00:14:44.276 --> 00:14:46.900
So in this case here, you'll see
we just copy in an application

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.780
that we've built. We
take a centos image.

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:50.530
We update it.

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:52.210
We install any of
the dependencies

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:53.800
that we need for
that application.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:54.850
We run the RPM.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:56.770
And we instruct
it, when it starts,

00:14:56.770 --> 00:14:59.020
this is the application
that I want to run.

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:02.020
That's really all it takes
to get a container that

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:03.992
runs an application.

00:15:03.992 --> 00:15:05.950
It's probably going to
be bigger than you want,

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:07.870
because you're dragging
in a lot of things.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:08.860
But it will work.

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:12.290
And it will do what
you need to do.

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:15.240
The next step is changing how
you package your application.

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:19.190
So if we're just building an RPM
for the privilege of installing

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:21.560
it in a Docker container, we
don't really need that RPM.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.710
We should just build the
Docker container directly.

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:27.410
So we make whatever changes we
need here in this pseudo code

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:28.050
build script.

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:31.670
We'll check out the application,
build the release binaries.

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.580
And now instead of calling
the step to build an RPM,

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:36.860
we could say, take that
directory of output,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.420
and then build a
Docker container

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:41.300
from a file which is on top
of a generic Linux operating

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:43.190
system, whatever it
is that you need,

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:46.854
install and run these files.

00:15:46.854 --> 00:15:48.270
Now that's fantastic
when we start

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:50.640
looking at continuous
integration tools.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:54.340
So all modern CI systems
can build containers.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:55.770
This is Jenkins here.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:58.950
It's very easy to get to a
world where you commit a change

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.505
and have a container
automatically built.

00:16:01.505 --> 00:16:02.880
And the great
thing about Jenkins

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.980
is that you can use
the clusters that you

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:07.869
have to provide the compute
to run the builders that

00:16:07.869 --> 00:16:10.410
build the containers that will
then go run on these clusters.

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.060
So you can use the
empty space, again,

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:16.440
to increase the utilization
of the machines that you have.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:19.620
Alternatively, this week we've
launched Google Cloud Container

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.380
Builder, which is a hosted
service for building container

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:24.120
images in the cloud.

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:27.130
So again you have a choice,
do I just connect this up

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:29.310
so it can watch for
pushes to GitHub,

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.530
and then automatically build
containers, and put them

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.077
into my secure registry?

00:16:33.077 --> 00:16:35.160
Or is there something that
I want to run yourself?

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.201
You always have that
trade-off between the things

00:16:37.201 --> 00:16:39.844
you want to manage versus
taking on a managed service.

00:16:39.844 --> 00:16:41.760
And I do encourage you
to have a look at that.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:48.230
So with no changes
to the code at all,

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:51.470
we've gone from a situation
where people say, ah, well,

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:52.520
it worked on my machine.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:53.936
Why doesn't it
work in production?

00:16:53.936 --> 00:16:55.160
And it's so hard to deploy.

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:57.160
And rolling back
is very difficult,

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:00.170
to having an artifact
which runs the same any way

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:03.650
you run sitting on top of
the container run time.

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:05.780
You just start the
container using a command

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:07.740
like Docker run to deploy it.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:10.400
And then if you need to
roll back to an old image,

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:12.980
all you need to do is
restart the old image.

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:14.139
You have them both there.

00:17:14.139 --> 00:17:16.430
You don't have to think, oh,
I've upgraded the machine.

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:18.263
I now have to downgrade
it again-- or worse,

00:17:18.263 --> 00:17:19.880
redeploy the entire machine--

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:21.170
to do a safe rollback.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.925
Now let's look at
the clustering.

00:17:26.925 --> 00:17:28.800
So if you remember the
title of this session,

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.300
the two things we're
looking for are

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:32.160
utilization and portability.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:35.070
So we'll take what we have
that runs on a single machine

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:37.170
and move it to run in a
multi-machine clustered

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.085
environment.

00:17:39.085 --> 00:17:40.636
The first thing I
suggest is if you

00:17:40.636 --> 00:17:43.260
haven't read "The Twelve-Factor
Manifesto" recently, do go back

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:43.935
and have a look at it.

00:17:43.935 --> 00:17:45.310
It was published
a few years ago.

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:47.100
But it really
boils down the idea

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.540
that to run these
scalable applications led

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.760
to the development
of cloud native.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:57.330
Your applications are probably
missing a few of these steps.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:58.920
So for example,
Twelve-Factor says

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:01.140
that the configuration
for your application

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:03.900
should be provided by the
environment that they run on,

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.239
rather than packed
into these containers.

00:18:06.239 --> 00:18:08.280
You don't want to have to
build a different image

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.410
to run in your
development environment

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:11.730
to run in production.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.360
What you should do is just
say, in that container,

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.380
fetch from an
environment variable--

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:18.450
or as a command line
flag, for example--

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:19.440
the configuration.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.594
And then have the
environment provide that.

00:18:22.594 --> 00:18:24.510
In the Kubernetes case,
we have an abstraction

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.280
called Secrets, which
are abstractions

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.440
for simple credential
management.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.350
We also have config
maps, which are a way

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:32.790
to parse configuration
into environments.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:35.460
And if you want, you can connect
up to more complex things

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.740
like a [? hasher ?] called
Vault, or our Cloud Key

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.619
Management Service,
to provide credentials

00:18:39.619 --> 00:18:41.910
and your applications, things
you definitely should not

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:43.451
be checking into
GitHub, for example.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:49.430
Given that we now have our
code and our configuration,

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:52.480
the next thing we want to do is
actually deploy an application.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:54.990
So we'll create a
deployment object.

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:56.800
And that will
create a replica set

00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:00.940
with all of the pods
having the same metadata.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:03.310
And we just specify how
many replicas we want.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:07.690
We should make that choice based
on the resource requirements.

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:09.640
And we encourage
you to test what

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:12.570
it is that you get when you have
a certain amount of CPU and RAM

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:15.400
per container because we want
to set those limits to make sure

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:19.790
that we don't run away with all
the free space in the cluster.

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:22.010
Then we create a
service, which groups

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:24.530
those pods together,
and potentially connect

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:28.220
that to something like an NGRES
to get inbound traffic in.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:32.150
And now we have our container
application inside our cluster

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:35.900
receiving traffic and serving.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:39.230
Having done all that, all of
these things are API-driven.

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:41.270
So we can lead to a
world where all of this

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:42.560
is done automatically for us.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:45.830
We can do continuous delivery
where you push your change.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:47.480
It gets built. It gets tested.

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:49.580
Perhaps there's a
human approval step.

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:54.320
And then the deployment
is done to the cluster.

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:57.460
And first, we'd like to use the
tool called Spinnaker, which

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:00.850
we've worked with Netflix,
who built this tool initially

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:04.437
as an enhancement on their
work on VM-based deployments.

00:20:04.437 --> 00:20:06.520
And we've especially
collaborated with them around

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:09.880
being able to do Kubernetes in
container-based deployments.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:11.530
But again, the APIs
are there, so you

00:20:11.530 --> 00:20:13.480
can tie this into whatever
continuous delivery

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:14.950
system you use.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.620
Or you can even run this from
the cloud container builder,

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:19.885
and just have a step
that does deployment.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:25.490
Now if you're running a common
application, the open source

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:27.980
databases, for example, you
think, well, I don't actually

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:29.570
need to go through
the effort of building

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:30.740
all of these containers myself.

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.270
Someone else has
probably done that.

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:33.987
And then I can just
reuse their work.

00:20:33.987 --> 00:20:35.820
So there's a package
manager for Kubernetes,

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:37.580
which is called Helm.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:39.440
It contains many
open source packages

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:40.950
that are ready to deploy.

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:43.730
But it's also an
engine you can use

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:46.310
to describe how your
containers, and your pods,

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:48.830
and services relate to each
other in an environment,

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:50.720
and then use that
as the thing that

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:52.390
gets deployed on your cluster.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.700
So similar to how a package
manager, like Docker RPM,

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:57.950
for example, installs the
thing on a single machine,

00:20:57.950 --> 00:20:59.533
Helm brings that up
a level, and says,

00:20:59.533 --> 00:21:01.259
install this on the
cluster wherever

00:21:01.259 --> 00:21:02.300
it happens to need to be.

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:09.260
So when we would think about
anything that handles states,

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:11.594
we need to think about storage.

00:21:11.594 --> 00:21:13.010
Traditionally,
what we would do is

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:14.867
we would have some
large set of disks,

00:21:14.867 --> 00:21:17.450
and a computer attached to them
that runs to the database that

00:21:17.450 --> 00:21:18.880
serves that data.

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.747
You'd say that the
work follows the state.

00:21:21.747 --> 00:21:23.580
Now we're talking about
distributed clusters

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:24.290
in Kubernetes.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:26.581
You should think about turning
that around, and saying,

00:21:26.581 --> 00:21:28.370
the state follows the work.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:31.580
So wherever your database
engine gets scheduled,

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.230
the state is just
attached by way

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:35.930
of taking a network disk that
contains that, putting it

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:38.180
on the right machine
automatically

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:41.950
as a volume, which is
then mapped into that pod.

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.510
If you think of a
use case where you

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.480
have a number of
different nodes which

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.707
run a set of replicas
for a database,

00:21:48.707 --> 00:21:50.290
they all have slightly
different data,

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:52.720
depending on what's
[? shouted ?] on each one.

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:55.210
And instead of just having a
replica set with each thing

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:56.710
having the same
data attached to it,

00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:59.500
we now have an abstraction
called a stateful set, which

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:05.200
lets you say, this particular
replica has disk zero, replica

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.570
zero, disk one, replica
one, for example--

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:09.845
map through to the number
that you need to have.

00:22:09.845 --> 00:22:12.220
And it's a great way for
running these stateful workloads

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:14.850
on top of Kubernetes.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:17.100
Where am I going
to store this data?

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:18.960
The great thing
about Kubernetes is

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.540
that you don't need to
think about the abstraction

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:24.120
below as someone who is
simply deploying and running

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:25.560
applications.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:28.650
Kubernetes abstracts
away the physicality

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.971
of the parts, the sort of
below the imaginary line.

00:22:31.971 --> 00:22:33.720
It might be that, in
your own data center,

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:37.390
you have network disks
attached by iSCSI.

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:40.930
If you run OpenStack, you
might use the Cinder plugins.

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:44.190
And on cloud, we have network
attachable persistent disks.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:46.630
So all you do is say,
Kubernetes, give me a volume.

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:49.140
Or you have a volume provision
by your administrator.

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:52.110
And it is backed by whatever is
available in your environment.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.510
So again, you don't have to
write different code to run

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:56.026
in different environments.

00:22:56.026 --> 00:22:57.400
So you end up with
an application

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:02.400
which is eminently portable
because of that consistent API.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:03.806
Same is true of network.

00:23:03.806 --> 00:23:05.430
How am I going to
get incoming traffic?

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:07.805
Well, it might be that I have
physical load balances that

00:23:07.805 --> 00:23:10.020
might require a network
team or someone to program

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:12.410
before they work in my
physical environment.

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:14.160
But in the cloud
environment, I might just

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:18.590
be able to make an API call, and
then have that wired up for me.

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:20.800
The same things that are
true of these abstractions

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:24.725
are both storage and networking.

00:23:24.725 --> 00:23:26.100
Now that comes in
very handy when

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.500
we get to step three in our
three-step process, which is

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:32.080
actually moving to the cloud.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:34.900
In the simplest
case, we just take

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:36.400
the Kubernetes
configuration files

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:38.110
that we've built,
that we were running

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:42.310
on our on-premise environment,
we point our command line tool

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:43.640
to a cloud environment.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:45.874
And we do exactly
the same thing.

00:23:45.874 --> 00:23:47.290
It sounds flippant,
but I actually

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:49.081
sat down with a customer
at home in London.

00:23:49.081 --> 00:23:51.820
And we said, let's get your
app running on Google Container

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:52.960
Engine.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:54.430
It took them longer
to figure out

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:56.500
how to get their
laptop connected back

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:59.350
to remote into their desktop
from the conference room,

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:01.560
and then figure out what
environment variables they'd

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:03.820
set wrong, which meant that
none of the commands would run,

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:06.028
than it did to actually get
their environment running

00:24:06.028 --> 00:24:07.940
on the cloud.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:11.252
That's the magic of possibility.

00:24:11.252 --> 00:24:12.710
Now, of course,
there is more to it

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:16.460
than this, because you need to
synchronize your data, as well.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:19.220
And you need to generally have a
hybrid environment where you're

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:20.960
running while that
process is happening

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:23.000
across multiple places.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:25.520
We're working on that
Federated API servers,

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:28.130
so we have the facility
to have clusters

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:32.446
that run in different places
managed in the same way.

00:24:32.446 --> 00:24:34.070
And then it really
just comes up to you

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:36.740
to be able to handle
the migration of data

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:39.110
by whichever way makes the
most sense for whatever it

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:40.190
is that you're running.

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:42.065
So stateless workloads,
you don't necessarily

00:24:42.065 --> 00:24:43.470
have any data to migrate.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:47.130
But when we start talking
about storage, you do.

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:48.630
So now I'd like to
invite our guests

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:51.088
from Samsung SDS, who are going
to talk through a migration

00:24:51.088 --> 00:24:52.110
that they did.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:53.610
Bob Wise is the group's CTO.

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:55.485
And he's going to talk
a little bit about how

00:24:55.485 --> 00:24:56.916
customers decide when to move.

00:24:56.916 --> 00:24:58.290
And again, if
you're a developer,

00:24:58.290 --> 00:24:59.940
this is something you can
take back to your boss,

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:02.580
and say, these are things that
we really need to think about,

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:04.832
and that will let me do
these cool deployments.

00:25:04.832 --> 00:25:06.540
And then Lee Chang is
a senior architect.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:08.460
And he's going to talk
about the technical details

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:10.209
of the particular
migration that they did.

00:25:10.209 --> 00:25:12.669
So thank you very much to Bob.

00:25:12.669 --> 00:25:15.627
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:17.489 --> 00:25:19.530
BOB WISE: I think there
is a lot of good material

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:21.150
in those slides.

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:23.850
Hopefully, we'll be able
to help you instantiate

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:25.736
a bit by going through
a real-life example

00:25:25.736 --> 00:25:26.860
that we've been working on.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:34.550
So Lee and I work for Samsung
SDS, the Cloud Native Computing

00:25:34.550 --> 00:25:36.670
team.

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:38.000
So what does that mean?

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.060
I should say, first of
all, even though I'm

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:43.910
on the board of the
CNCF, and our group

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:45.495
is all about cloud
native, there's

00:25:45.495 --> 00:25:47.870
something I don't like about
the cloud native word, which

00:25:47.870 --> 00:25:49.910
is it has this strong
implication that you have

00:25:49.910 --> 00:25:53.930
to build things from scratch,
that they have to be natively

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:56.255
engineered and designed
for that kind of cloud

00:25:56.255 --> 00:25:57.586
native environment.

00:25:57.586 --> 00:25:58.960
And we don't
believe that's true.

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:01.126
And the example that we're
going to talk about today

00:26:01.126 --> 00:26:03.710
is a great example
of how is it that you

00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:08.630
can take existing
applications and migrate them

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:11.340
without a lot of re-architecture
into this new world.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.400
So there's a few
things we believe

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.220
that are very important.

00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:19.550
We are obsessive
about automation.

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:23.330
That's an important piece
of being cloud native.

00:26:23.330 --> 00:26:27.440
We're very dedicated,
and believe

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:34.320
that open source is really where
infrastructure is being built.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:37.640
And we're motivated by being
involved in that community.

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:41.480
Within Samsung Group, we
work with various teams

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:43.719
to help them with
their transformations.

00:26:43.719 --> 00:26:45.260
But we also have an
outside business.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:47.870
And Lee's going to have a little
bit longer part of the talk.

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:51.234
We're going to talk about one
of our customers engagements.

00:26:51.234 --> 00:26:53.150
A couple of things we're
going to talk about--

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:55.025
so I talked about cloud
native a bit already.

00:26:55.025 --> 00:26:57.320
I want to introduce this term
cluster ops, which you're

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:01.910
going to start hearing more,
which is this new term that's

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:04.550
trying to capture that there
are some new things that are

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:07.430
happening, not just
with the technology,

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:09.530
but with the organization.

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:11.870
How do companies organize
properly around this?

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:14.180
They will frequently
need some help

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:17.240
because this is a
big transformation.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:20.852
The thing that companies get
out of this kind of cloud

00:27:20.852 --> 00:27:23.390
native work is,
although, you can

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:25.310
use Kubernetes and
Containers to make

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:28.620
much more efficient use of
your infrastructure, most

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:29.120
companies--

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.070
Google might be an
example that spends

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.810
enormous amounts of money
on their infrastructure.

00:27:33.810 --> 00:27:37.340
But most companies spend
more money on their people.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:39.740
And it's much more important
to have great product

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:41.830
velocity and high quality.

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:45.860
The kind of infrastructure
optimization

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:48.950
is a less important priority.

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:53.067
So cluster ops is three things.

00:27:53.067 --> 00:27:54.650
How do you manage
your infrastructure?

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:56.720
How do you manage your org?

00:27:56.720 --> 00:28:00.230
How do you manage your tooling?

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:02.979
Deep embedded in that concept
is the notion of an SRE.

00:28:02.979 --> 00:28:04.520
And now we're getting
into these kind

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:07.310
of more complex
organizational topics that

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:08.570
are part of what we help with.

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:10.010
So we do executive consulting.

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:12.230
We do professional
services for migration.

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:14.200
And we help companies
adopt cluster ops.

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:19.870
So we've been contributors
involved in the Kubernetes

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:22.390
community for a long
time at this point.

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:24.890
We were on stage with Google
for the Kubernetes 1.0 launch.

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:26.620
We made a very early bet.

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:27.830
Why did we make that bet?

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:29.040
Was it a good bet?

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:32.010
So, yes, it was a great bet.

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:34.750
Kubernetes has become the
fastest-growing, best-supported

00:28:34.750 --> 00:28:37.464
orchestration system out there.

00:28:37.464 --> 00:28:39.130
It has the best options
for multi-cloud.

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:40.580
Craig mentioned that.

00:28:40.580 --> 00:28:43.000
And it's open source.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:45.040
Open source is good,
but it also comes

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:48.760
with some commitment that's
required in order to really

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:50.050
adopt it well.

00:28:50.050 --> 00:28:54.610
And if you're going to use
open source the way we do,

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:57.260
deep industry participation
is really important.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:29:00.160
So the way we do that
is we're involved

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:03.790
in helping run the
Kubernetes SIGs.

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:07.430
I'm on the CNCF board.

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:10.960
We have contributors
on the team.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:15.460
Now some of our customers don't
have the time, the energy,

00:29:15.460 --> 00:29:17.870
the capacity to be
engaged at that level.

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:20.980
And this is a way that we help.

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:24.580
So I'll also do a quick mention,
if you go to our GitHub,

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:27.190
we do all of our work
in open source, as well.

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:28.930
We have a project
called K2, which

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:31.480
is a lot of the provisioning
management tooling

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:32.820
that we built around Kubernetes.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.210
So what drives an
enterprise-wide transformation?

00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:42.020
So Craig mentioned that
if you're a developer,

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:42.930
come talk to us.

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:44.840
We can help you figure
out how to do this.

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:47.250
But probably most
of our engagement--

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:50.300
and that is help we provide.

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:52.820
How do you understand how to
explain this to your boss?

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:55.350
What's going to be the
effect on the organization?

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:57.710
Do you need validation
that companies like Samsung

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.300
are using this technology?

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:03.290
Most of our engagement,
though, go top down,

00:30:03.290 --> 00:30:07.000
where the C-suite is
starting to wonder, well,

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:10.580
why do I feel like my
competitors are going faster

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:11.600
than I am?

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:13.820
Can we save money with
container packing?

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:17.420
Do I have a modern team?

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:18.980
Do people really
want to work here

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:22.095
because we're using the best
and most modern technology?

00:30:22.095 --> 00:30:24.470
And why is it that it seems
like other people are growing

00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:25.820
faster as I read about it?

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:28.250
So consultants will come in.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:29.756
It's pretty easy
to find consultants

00:30:29.756 --> 00:30:32.130
that will come in and help
you with this kind of problem.

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:35.960
And most consulting
groups will offer

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:39.380
a couple of solutions straight
out of the consulting 101

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:40.670
playbook.

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:43.460
Build a massive parallel team.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:46.560
Let's help you hire some new
leadership into your team.

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:48.600
Let's completely
re-architect the system.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:54.979
This was my slight objection
to the cloud native term.

00:30:54.979 --> 00:30:56.520
But we've taken a
different approach.

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:02.830
I would say, the way I would
characterize our approach,

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:07.570
generally, is an
ops-first approach.

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:10.450
It's frequent that we
see agile transformation

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:12.520
kinds of projects
go a bit sideways

00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:15.910
because the
development teams get

00:31:15.910 --> 00:31:17.519
into this really rapid pace.

00:31:17.519 --> 00:31:19.060
But then, because
they can't actually

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:22.360
get their applications all
the way out into production,

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.790
that it all gets
wadded up around that.

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:28.390
So we prefer to
think of operations

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:30.897
as being the place to start
with this kind of technology.

00:31:30.897 --> 00:31:32.980
We will talk about our
engagement here a bit more.

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:37.210
So the customer we're
going to talk about today

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:39.790
is a company called Zonar.

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:43.540
They are in the
industrial IOT space,

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:46.720
have a long-running
business, lots and lots

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:49.932
of devices, lots and
lots of customers.

00:31:49.932 --> 00:31:52.140
And again, Lee's going to
talk about that a bit more.

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.850
But to give you a sense for--

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:56.710
like, how does a
project like this really

00:31:56.710 --> 00:31:59.320
get started from
the executive level?

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:01.960
The place that we decided
would be a great place for us

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:05.980
to engage is around
disaster recovery refresh

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:09.190
in the light of
public cloud options.

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:12.670
So we said, we'll analyze
those options for you.

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:14.100
We'll look at risks.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:15.940
We'll embed our
team with your team

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:18.390
to make sure we really
understand what's going on.

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:20.770
We'll build some
small experiments.

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:23.050
And then we'll re-plan
based on doing that.

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:27.010
So with that, Lee, why
don't you come on up?

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:32.410
And I don't know if you
want to leave slide here.

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:33.976
Great.

00:32:33.976 --> 00:32:34.840
Thanks.

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:35.710
LEE CHANG: Hello.

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:36.310
Thanks, Bob.

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:37.240
And thanks, Craig.

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:38.531
I know we keep shifting around.

00:32:38.531 --> 00:32:41.170
So I hope you guys could
keep track of who's who.

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:43.810
So I know you all were
listening really intently

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:47.350
to what Craig talked about
with regards to containers

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:49.180
and running services
in Kubernetes.

00:32:49.180 --> 00:32:51.013
Because what I'm going
to be talking about--

00:32:51.013 --> 00:32:52.510
and convince you,
hopefully, that--

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:53.950
taking these concepts,
you can actually

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:55.040
do it in the real world.

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:58.660
And what we did was Zonar, which
is what Bob mentioned, was we

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:01.030
analyzed-- so I don't know
if Bob talked about this.

00:33:01.030 --> 00:33:04.385
But what our project was was to
do a disaster recovery refresh

00:33:04.385 --> 00:33:05.125
with Zonar.

00:33:05.125 --> 00:33:07.000
They had systems that
were currently running.

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.520
They were looking at probably
modernizing the systems--

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:11.560
I should probably put
this in my pocket--

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:13.660
and also trying to
see what they needed

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:16.750
to do to add to their
business continuity plan.

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:19.240
One of the things that they
wanted to bring to the table

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:21.450
was potentially
moving to the cloud.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:24.909
At Samsung CNCT, we have a
lot of engineers on staff

00:33:24.909 --> 00:33:26.950
who've actually have done
a lot of lift and shift

00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:31.780
from on-prem systems into
the cloud, VMs, containers.

00:33:31.780 --> 00:33:33.820
Like Bob mentioned, we've
been doing Kubernetes

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:34.670
since the get go.

00:33:34.670 --> 00:33:37.417
So it's been about
two-some odd years

00:33:37.417 --> 00:33:39.250
that we're working with
Kubernetes, and also

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:39.757
containers.

00:33:39.757 --> 00:33:41.590
We did containers,
actually, a few years ago

00:33:41.590 --> 00:33:43.382
before that with Cloud Foundry.

00:33:43.382 --> 00:33:45.590
There could be arguments if
that's containers or not.

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:47.780
But that's moving small
packets into the cloud.

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:49.510
So we have a lot of
experience with that.

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:53.440
And Zonar, knowing that we were
such subject-matter experts,

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:56.245
they brought us in, had us look
over some of their systems that

00:33:56.245 --> 00:33:58.120
were running in their
secondary data center--

00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:00.720
which was their disaster
recovery system--

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:03.332
and give them some options on
potentially moving that system

00:34:03.332 --> 00:34:05.290
to the cloud-- not all
of it, maybe part of it.

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:08.260
But what we did was,
like Bob mentioned,

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:11.094
we had our teams of
embedded with their teams.

00:34:11.094 --> 00:34:12.760
This was a nice place
to be because they

00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:15.250
were only two blocks away
from our office in Seattle.

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:16.833
It's not that we
don't like traveling,

00:34:16.833 --> 00:34:18.862
but it's great to be able
to just go down there.

00:34:18.862 --> 00:34:20.320
If you needed to
do deep dives, you

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:23.020
need to analyze their systems,
is just two blocks away.

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:25.630
So we could go over
there for five minutes.

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:27.760
What we did was did some POCs.

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:30.001
We looked at their stack.

00:34:30.001 --> 00:34:31.750
We made sure that what
we were going to do

00:34:31.750 --> 00:34:33.320
was always going to be feasible.

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:35.189
So mostly what we're
looking out for was,

00:34:35.189 --> 00:34:37.480
did they have any proprietary
systems that prevent them

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:38.679
from moving to the clouds?

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:41.230
So if they needed network
equipment that was proprietary,

00:34:41.230 --> 00:34:43.767
or they needed systems that
needed to be run on bare metal.

00:34:43.767 --> 00:34:45.100
Luckily, none of that was there.

00:34:45.100 --> 00:34:47.199
So it was a lot simpler
story to say that we

00:34:47.199 --> 00:34:48.639
could move to containers.

00:34:48.639 --> 00:34:51.469
Since we are here to listen to
people talk about containers

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:53.910
and Kubernetes, the
question wasn't if they

00:34:53.910 --> 00:34:54.909
could move to the cloud.

00:34:54.909 --> 00:34:56.920
The question actually was,
where can you run Kubernetes?

00:34:56.920 --> 00:34:58.360
Because we went
through the steps.

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:00.920
We figured that containers
was a viable option for them.

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:02.870
So where can we run Kubernetes?

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:04.090
Kubernetes is in our DNA.

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:06.880
We've worked with
Google since day one.

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:08.900
They were already using
Google Container Engine.

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:10.970
I forgot to tell you,
this was about a year ago.

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:12.470
So we followed
closely that project.

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:13.990
And we realized,
this is probably

00:35:13.990 --> 00:35:16.690
the best place for them
to move their systems

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:19.180
for the secondary data center.

00:35:19.180 --> 00:35:21.494
Part of the benefits
of using GKE--

00:35:21.494 --> 00:35:23.410
since we've been using
Kubernetes for a while,

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:26.240
we understand any cluster
that you want to operate,

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:28.510
there's operational
loads, start-up costs,

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:30.010
putting your staff
through training

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:32.260
to understand how you're
going to run a new cluster.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:34.136
It's different than what
they had in on-prem.

00:35:34.136 --> 00:35:35.635
And the other thing
is, what are you

00:35:35.635 --> 00:35:37.330
going to do for
building a new cluster?

00:35:37.330 --> 00:35:40.230
They had business that
were running currently

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:42.780
servicing customer
needs, bringing in money.

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:44.335
How are they going
to extend that?

00:35:44.335 --> 00:35:46.960
This is what they were trying to
do with their new data center.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:48.085
They needed to extend that.

00:35:48.085 --> 00:35:49.430
They were getting more business.

00:35:49.430 --> 00:35:52.230
So how do you not increase
more in your capital costs?

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:56.087
So Kubernetes, again, with GKE
was a great solution for that.

00:35:56.087 --> 00:35:58.670
And another thing that they had
with-- after many, many years,

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:01.420
15 plus years, of operating
a data center on-prem,

00:36:01.420 --> 00:36:02.960
they had expertise to do that.

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:08.287
So GKE is using Kubernetes
underneath any place

00:36:08.287 --> 00:36:10.370
that you can run Kubernetes,
whether it be on-prem

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:11.810
or in the cloud.

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:14.420
So we can use Kubernetes
running on GKE very easily.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:19.470
A little bit of context of
what the customer was running.

00:36:19.470 --> 00:36:21.870
It was a classic N-tier
system, three tiers.

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:23.940
You had the web tier
application tier.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:26.377
You had the middle tier,
and a database back end.

00:36:26.377 --> 00:36:28.710
The database transactions,
or the business transactions,

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:31.560
were spread across multiple
PostgreSQL database servers.

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:34.240
They had about 100
database servers running.

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:37.720
All their application systems,
and middle tier and databases

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:39.290
systems were running on VM.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.600
90% of it was VMs all running
on bare metal in their data

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:45.080
center, and also in their
secondary data center.

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:47.520
They had a large installed
base of head units.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.880
So their business was
running head units, 450,000,

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:53.910
500,000 head units that
were basically IOT systems.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:56.700
And what they did was take
sensory data, telemetry data,

00:36:56.700 --> 00:36:59.050
sent that back through the
internet to their data center

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:02.170
to do the processing and
do reports off of that.

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:04.810
The traffic that was
resulting from that

00:37:04.810 --> 00:37:07.450
was all run through
their network appliances.

00:37:07.450 --> 00:37:09.850
So the load balancing
network route switchers

00:37:09.850 --> 00:37:12.040
were all hardwared running
in their data centers.

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:15.220
And the majority of all their
data that was landing on files

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:17.770
was all stored on network
attach source systems.

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:21.790
And 200 terabytes
of data was being

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:25.240
stored remotely from
their database servers

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:28.000
in their on-prem data centers.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:30.940
So again, it was pointed
out that their dev

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:34.330
teams were excited about
moving forward fast.

00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:37.450
Some of them had
experience with containers.

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:39.290
So there was a big
drive to say, hey,

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:40.998
if we're going to move
forward, let's see

00:37:40.998 --> 00:37:42.970
if we can use containers
in the new solution

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:44.460
that we're providing for them.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:45.460
So that is a great plus.

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:47.710
We didn't have to do a lot
of convincing on their side

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:50.980
to get them to move to a
container-type environment.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:53.230
So what we came up with, a
model that we can go with--

00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:54.560
and I forgot my pointer.

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:56.650
So I'm going to have to
do a lot hand waving here.

00:37:56.650 --> 00:37:58.810
Their current system,
their current data center,

00:37:58.810 --> 00:38:01.630
and their secondary center,
consisted of the head units--

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:04.630
400,000 to 500,000 head units
that go through the internet

00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:07.090
and send their telemetry
data to their current data

00:38:07.090 --> 00:38:08.477
center in Seattle.

00:38:08.477 --> 00:38:09.560
So they had their gateway.

00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:11.850
The load balancers were
all physical appliances.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:14.410
Web servers running on bare
metal, and VMs, and database

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:15.100
servers.

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:19.150
So the new system using
the Google Direct Connect

00:38:19.150 --> 00:38:22.840
through our partner, which
was a 10 GigE low latency,

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:26.305
since we're using the
central location, about 40

00:38:26.305 --> 00:38:29.710
to 50 milliseconds connected
to the Cloud Connect using

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:31.026
cloud load balancing.

00:38:31.026 --> 00:38:33.400
If any of you have used
Kubernetes and Angular, you all

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:34.900
realize, if you
want services that

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:36.629
need to talk to a
Kubernetes resource,

00:38:36.629 --> 00:38:37.920
you need to use a load balance.

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.470
So Cloud Load Balancer was
a great solution for that.

00:38:41.470 --> 00:38:43.560
Container engines-- since
this is a GKE solution,

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:45.070
we used Container Engine.

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:47.440
Persistent disk to backup
back any systems that

00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:48.760
needed stateful files.

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:52.210
And we use Google Container
Registry for all the Docker

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:54.940
images that were built.
It's a great product.

00:38:54.940 --> 00:38:58.000
It's co-load with your cluster,
so you get really fast response

00:38:58.000 --> 00:38:58.519
times.

00:38:58.519 --> 00:39:00.560
And also we were able to
leverage the IAM system,

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:04.250
so the security
was there built in.

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:06.980
The process that we did to move
them from their current data

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:09.600
center, or their secondary data
center and their primary data

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:11.940
centers, to the cloud, we
used a lot of our own tools,

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:16.470
called K2, K2-CI, which is
our automatic delivery system.

00:39:16.470 --> 00:39:18.360
Those consisted of other tools.

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:19.990
We took a lot of
open source projects.

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:22.170
We also used Google's Cloud CLI.

00:39:22.170 --> 00:39:24.947
And brought that up to do
some lifecycle management.

00:39:24.947 --> 00:39:27.030
One of the biggest tools
that we use from Google--

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:28.980
and we got this sort of
free, free as in beer.

00:39:28.980 --> 00:39:31.105
You still have to pay for
some of the transactions.

00:39:31.105 --> 00:39:33.300
But we leveraged the
ability to use Stackdriver

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:35.024
for logging and monitoring.

00:39:35.024 --> 00:39:37.440
This actually contributed to
reducing the operational load

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.540
on the teams, in that
they didn't have to create

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:41.475
another monitoring system.

00:39:41.475 --> 00:39:43.350
Now there's going to be
some swivel chairing.

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:44.490
People are going
argue, well, you've

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:46.364
got some swivel chairing
went back and forth.

00:39:46.364 --> 00:39:48.600
But the benefits of having
a system that's pre-built,

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:52.230
that's running automatically--
there's like a flag or some

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:53.850
command line you
could add to it--

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:56.430
and able to get all
the feedback real time.

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:59.600
And also, monitoring
was a big benefit.

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:02.000
As mentioned before, automation,
automation, automation

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.350
is really important.

00:40:03.350 --> 00:40:06.034
We used a pipeline called CAIC--

00:40:06.034 --> 00:40:07.200
we need a new name for this.

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:10.790
I'm sorry-- K2-CI
Application Delivery Pipeline

00:40:10.790 --> 00:40:13.440
to do our automated building,
and testing, and deployment

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:14.990
of applications to GKE.

00:40:14.990 --> 00:40:18.140
So what we started with was
production-quality artifacts

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:22.340
from Zonar, binaries
that were already going

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:23.990
to their production systems.

00:40:23.990 --> 00:40:25.910
We took any changes
from there and put them

00:40:25.910 --> 00:40:27.390
into this deployment pipeline.

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:28.640
We could also use source code.

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:30.980
But in this case, we used
their binary artifacts

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:33.680
from their artifact system and
packaged that with Docker files

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:34.670
and Helm charts.

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:37.220
So Craig talked about
Helm charts back

00:40:37.220 --> 00:40:38.810
in his previous talk.

00:40:38.810 --> 00:40:41.180
We use Helm charts as
the delivery mechanism.

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:43.190
So everything was
wrapped in Helm charts

00:40:43.190 --> 00:40:44.744
that's within the source code.

00:40:44.744 --> 00:40:46.910
Any changes there were
reflected in the Helm charts.

00:40:46.910 --> 00:40:48.740
And actually, what
Helm charts provide

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:50.690
you is the ability to
say, this is the state

00:40:50.690 --> 00:40:53.870
that I expect Kubernetes to be
running this application, which

00:40:53.870 --> 00:40:55.520
is really nice for us.

00:40:55.520 --> 00:40:59.270
We also use Helm charts to
codify manage services outside

00:40:59.270 --> 00:41:03.550
of Google Container
Engine itself.

00:41:03.550 --> 00:41:06.092
We have dependent resources that
are running in Google Cloud.

00:41:06.092 --> 00:41:07.883
We didn't want to have
a separate mechanism

00:41:07.883 --> 00:41:09.260
or a configuration
system that we

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:11.960
needed to run in addition
to our pipeline system.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:13.580
So we actually
codify and managed

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:15.200
that in Kubernetes charts.

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:19.110
So those usually included, the
cloud load-balancer solution,

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:25.367
the networking cloud solution,
and the cloud DNS solution

00:41:25.367 --> 00:41:26.450
that's provided by Google.

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:28.430
So those were all managed
to Helm charts, which really

00:41:28.430 --> 00:41:29.881
simplified the pipeline itself.

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:35.130
Database, so I know
Craig, in his slide,

00:41:35.130 --> 00:41:36.857
had a big, red
maybe on databases.

00:41:36.857 --> 00:41:38.690
We've been doing this
for a number of years.

00:41:38.690 --> 00:41:40.564
Our teams have done a
lot of lifts and shifts

00:41:40.564 --> 00:41:43.420
on open source databases,
from MySQL to PostgreSQL.

00:41:43.420 --> 00:41:46.632
And the answer to this
is, yes, you could do it.

00:41:46.632 --> 00:41:49.090
Previously, with VMs, it was
a little bit more complicated,

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:51.490
and sometimes more hairy with
the wiring and everything

00:41:51.490 --> 00:41:53.311
you had to do in the
back end systems.

00:41:53.311 --> 00:41:54.310
Actually, let me backup.

00:41:54.310 --> 00:41:57.130
So there was
questions asked to us,

00:41:57.130 --> 00:41:58.917
why don't we use
a hosted solution?

00:41:58.917 --> 00:42:01.000
Google announced their
PostgreSQL solution today--

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:02.022
yesterday.

00:42:02.022 --> 00:42:03.730
RDS is probably one
of the biggest things

00:42:03.730 --> 00:42:04.688
that people know about.

00:42:04.688 --> 00:42:06.580
They have PostgreSQL
running in RDS.

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:07.630
So why don't we use them?

00:42:07.630 --> 00:42:08.600
It's a Cadillac solution.

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:10.016
It gives you all
the nice benefits

00:42:10.016 --> 00:42:12.145
of redundancy and
automated failovers.

00:42:12.145 --> 00:42:14.020
The biggest reason
why is the customer,

00:42:14.020 --> 00:42:16.930
Zonar, actually uses
proprietary extensions.

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:18.760
Their business, a lot
of the domain logic,

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:20.860
uses proprietary
PostgreSQL extensions.

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:23.765
So asking their development
team to refactor that, put that

00:42:23.765 --> 00:42:26.140
into the middle tier, was
pretty much out of the question

00:42:26.140 --> 00:42:27.681
because they needed
this project done

00:42:27.681 --> 00:42:29.470
at a relatively rapid manner.

00:42:29.470 --> 00:42:31.810
So hosting and using
our own database

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:33.200
was really the
only path forward.

00:42:33.200 --> 00:42:36.130
So we said yes to
the question, can we

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:38.927
do the lift and shift
for the database server.

00:42:38.927 --> 00:42:40.760
This caused consternation,
a lot of debates.

00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:44.000
But we prototyped this
and proved that it worked.

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.660
And again, Kubernetes
really benefited us in this.

00:42:46.660 --> 00:42:49.390
We used stateful services
and Persistent Volumes

00:42:49.390 --> 00:42:51.937
using Persistent
disks off Google.

00:42:51.937 --> 00:42:53.770
Google-- I don't know
if you all know this--

00:42:53.770 --> 00:42:56.609
the bigger the disk, the better
IOPS, the better latency,

00:42:56.609 --> 00:42:58.900
and all that good stuff,
which was really nice compared

00:42:58.900 --> 00:43:00.067
to products out there--

00:43:00.067 --> 00:43:01.900
compared to other cloud
providers out there.

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:04.780
So you have your on-prem system
with the database running

00:43:04.780 --> 00:43:08.350
on bare metal, and
your data landing

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:10.270
on the network-attached storage.

00:43:10.270 --> 00:43:11.920
Or we had a stream
of application

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:14.680
going through the
internet over the big pipe

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:17.170
that we had configured and
provisioned with Google

00:43:17.170 --> 00:43:18.970
to Cloud Interconnect
Load Balancer.

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:22.270
So that could talk specifically
to one database server running

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:23.860
in a pod as a stateful pod.

00:43:23.860 --> 00:43:26.200
And that guaranteed that the
disk that eventually gets

00:43:26.200 --> 00:43:27.640
created with the data file--

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:29.360
if that pod goes
away, stateful service

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:30.860
brings it up and
does all the wiring

00:43:30.860 --> 00:43:32.607
for you, which made
life really simple.

00:43:32.607 --> 00:43:34.690
When you first try to do
these things, when you're

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:36.730
showing the customer that
this can take the load based

00:43:36.730 --> 00:43:38.729
on your calculations,
it's not going to do that.

00:43:38.729 --> 00:43:41.110
So if it doesn't do
it, five minutes later,

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:43.870
spin something up with a
bigger resource requirement.

00:43:43.870 --> 00:43:45.591
The quota gets
increased, and boom.

00:43:45.591 --> 00:43:47.090
You can just change
your wall files.

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:48.940
Everything just syncs
up, hopefully, magically.

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:50.140
But if it doesn't
work, you could

00:43:50.140 --> 00:43:51.348
spend another day doing that.

00:43:51.348 --> 00:43:53.950
Versus a VM, where you
have to reconfigure,

00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:56.350
rewire all the database
files in the back end.

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:59.290
So this really helped us
show that the customer can

00:43:59.290 --> 00:44:03.560
run their workload over in
the cloud fairly simply.

00:44:03.560 --> 00:44:06.170
The pipeline itself, now this
is not a literal representation

00:44:06.170 --> 00:44:07.610
of our pipeline.

00:44:07.610 --> 00:44:10.760
If I had that chart up here,
it if even was landscaped,

00:44:10.760 --> 00:44:13.822
it would be wall-to-wall,
a bunch of flows,

00:44:13.822 --> 00:44:15.780
and a bunch of arrows
going all over the place.

00:44:15.780 --> 00:44:17.810
So this is a very
high-level, probably

00:44:17.810 --> 00:44:20.220
100,000 [? file ?] view.

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:22.270
The way it worked was--

00:44:22.270 --> 00:44:23.630
I wish I had my pointer.

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:28.190
So we took production artifacts
from their systems on-prem.

00:44:28.190 --> 00:44:30.710
Any changes there, the
pipeline picked it up,

00:44:30.710 --> 00:44:32.690
pushed that change
through Docker Build

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:35.150
into the Google
Container Registry.

00:44:35.150 --> 00:44:37.130
That updates the tag
inside the Helm chart.

00:44:37.130 --> 00:44:38.670
And there might be other
changes to Helm chart,

00:44:38.670 --> 00:44:40.700
but we'll simplify that
and say there isn't.

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:44.420
Once those changes are
detected, we do a Helm install,

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:45.440
the pipeline does.

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:47.600
It goes into a
staging environment.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:50.030
The staging environment
is created on demand.

00:44:50.030 --> 00:44:51.620
So that way, if you
need another test,

00:44:51.620 --> 00:44:53.453
you're not going to
pollute that environment

00:44:53.453 --> 00:44:54.860
with the previous test.

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:56.510
If the staging
environment test passed,

00:44:56.510 --> 00:44:57.800
the conformance test passes.

00:44:57.800 --> 00:44:59.960
And if this is the one
that goes to production,

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:00.917
you do a Helm upgrade.

00:45:00.917 --> 00:45:03.500
And the reason why you do a Helm
upgrade versus a Helm install

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:05.900
is you don't want to affect
any resources or objects that

00:45:05.900 --> 00:45:07.650
are already running
on that cluster.

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:09.530
Helm [? uninstall ?] only
does changes inside opponent.

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:11.696
So it only makes changes
to things that are actually

00:45:11.696 --> 00:45:15.060
changed within either the Helm
chart, or the artifact itself.

00:45:15.060 --> 00:45:18.560
So if you have load balancers,
you have stateful services.

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:20.240
And if you have
applications out there,

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:21.323
it'll do a rolling update.

00:45:21.323 --> 00:45:23.810
But the load balancers and
network connection DNSs,

00:45:23.810 --> 00:45:25.849
those don't get
changed or get deleted

00:45:25.849 --> 00:45:27.890
if those changes aren't
reflected inside the Helm

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:28.390
chart.

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:34.780
So some of the things that we
ran into during this process,

00:45:34.780 --> 00:45:37.540
with any type of trying
to understand applications

00:45:37.540 --> 00:45:39.490
from the outside, it
could take a while

00:45:39.490 --> 00:45:41.490
to figure out the
domain logic, also

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:43.640
what replicating applications.

00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:46.380
One of the things that
we also ran into--

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:48.130
but this is not something
that we've never

00:45:48.130 --> 00:45:50.710
seen before-- is VMs
could become bloated.

00:45:50.710 --> 00:45:52.360
You get monoliths at times.

00:45:52.360 --> 00:45:53.860
They had the classic
monolith, where

00:45:53.860 --> 00:45:55.551
they had database is running--

00:45:55.551 --> 00:45:56.050
I'm sorry.

00:45:56.050 --> 00:45:58.549
In the VM, they actually had
databases running their caching

00:45:58.549 --> 00:45:59.129
solutions.

00:45:59.129 --> 00:46:00.545
And they had web
applications that

00:46:00.545 --> 00:46:03.430
were run, Nginx,
Tomcat, RabbitMQ,

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:05.890
and their Memcache systems.

00:46:05.890 --> 00:46:10.722
We, with Kubernetes, was able
to refactor that pretty quickly,

00:46:10.722 --> 00:46:12.430
put the applications
in single container,

00:46:12.430 --> 00:46:14.660
and just use pods to manage
that, do all the wiring,

00:46:14.660 --> 00:46:17.059
and the networking for that.

00:46:17.059 --> 00:46:18.850
There's also some lack
of management tools.

00:46:18.850 --> 00:46:20.150
Kubernetes is
still pretty young.

00:46:20.150 --> 00:46:21.191
It's about two years old.

00:46:21.191 --> 00:46:23.860
So given any new
project, there might

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:25.880
be a lack of tools of
lifecycle management.

00:46:25.880 --> 00:46:28.570
But we have K2 with the years
of experience that we've had.

00:46:28.570 --> 00:46:30.335
We have K2, K2-CI.

00:46:30.335 --> 00:46:32.560
So we have those tools
already built in-house.

00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:35.140
That leverages a lot of
open source tools there.

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:36.860
Some of the wins that
Zonar experienced

00:46:36.860 --> 00:46:39.380
doing the [? post ?]
process was they

00:46:39.380 --> 00:46:43.090
are able to use the pipeline
in conjunction with GKE

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:46.200
and really speed up their
productivity, not just with

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:47.920
the disaster recovery solution.

00:46:47.920 --> 00:46:49.780
They were able to get
containers out faster,

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:51.880
databases out faster,
changing their schemas.

00:46:51.880 --> 00:46:53.950
Everything was just
a lot more quicker.

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:55.630
GKE also reduced the
operational load.

00:46:55.630 --> 00:46:57.850
I think I covered
that a bunch of times.

00:46:57.850 --> 00:47:00.940
Not having to operate
a cluster by hand,

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:03.190
we were able to depend on
Google's-- or Zonar was able

00:47:03.190 --> 00:47:06.640
to depend on Google's Site
Reliability engineers to do

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:09.160
the control plane act
management itself.

00:47:09.160 --> 00:47:10.870
And containerizing
their applications,

00:47:10.870 --> 00:47:14.300
they experienced better
scalability running on GKE.

00:47:14.300 --> 00:47:16.840
Scalability-- and not that
when disaster happens,

00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:19.797
and they move all their
traffic to the cloud,

00:47:19.797 --> 00:47:21.880
and they cannot respond
faster to production load,

00:47:21.880 --> 00:47:24.154
but they can also now be
portable across different

00:47:24.154 --> 00:47:24.820
infrastructures.

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:26.510
So they can run that
container-- kind

00:47:26.510 --> 00:47:29.091
of what Craig described-- one
application that's running

00:47:29.091 --> 00:47:30.340
in the container in the cloud.

00:47:30.340 --> 00:47:33.100
They could run locally on
their own personal laptops

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:34.210
using Minikube.

00:47:34.210 --> 00:47:36.160
Or they could also
run on on-prem systems

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:39.370
if they have a Kubernetes
cluster running on-prem.

00:47:39.370 --> 00:47:42.460
And they can also run in
other hosting solutions,

00:47:42.460 --> 00:47:44.870
and/or they could run another
cluster in themselves.

00:47:44.870 --> 00:47:46.300
GKE is really easy to start.

00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:48.760
Two command line commands,
boom, you got your own cluster

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:49.800
up and running.

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:50.830
Do another Helm install.

00:47:50.830 --> 00:47:53.110
Helm install is available
through the repo.

00:47:53.110 --> 00:47:55.660
And you could deploy another
environment pretty much

00:47:55.660 --> 00:47:58.140
at whim.

00:47:58.140 --> 00:48:01.020
Some of the takeaways I
hope you get from this,

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:03.720
you can adapt any
application stack--

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:06.960
and I'm using the word
any-- to move to the cloud

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:08.692
without major refactoring.

00:48:08.692 --> 00:48:10.650
And some of the benefits
that we went through--

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:13.170
and I went through
this really fast,

00:48:13.170 --> 00:48:16.770
but you can run GKE and
on-prem in a hybrid model.

00:48:16.770 --> 00:48:19.590
So you don't have to lift and
shift everything all at once.

00:48:19.590 --> 00:48:21.711
You can have services
running in your on-prem

00:48:21.711 --> 00:48:23.460
and have services
running in a cloud still

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:25.043
talking to your
on-prem as long as you

00:48:25.043 --> 00:48:27.356
have a nice direct
connect provisioned.

00:48:27.356 --> 00:48:28.730
Or we actually
have VPNs running.

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:31.050
So as long as it's not
full database traffic,

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:34.890
you're able to actually run it
in a hybrid model using GKE.

00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:37.740
Classic IT and Cloud
Native can be on the cloud.

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:40.260
So both systems, you can
definitely run in the cloud.

00:48:40.260 --> 00:48:44.030
And originally, when we were
first doing some cost analysis,

00:48:44.030 --> 00:48:48.870
this project versus them
doing a data center refresh

00:48:48.870 --> 00:48:50.700
in their secondary
data center, what

00:48:50.700 --> 00:48:52.560
would the costs
of spanning that,

00:48:52.560 --> 00:48:55.050
refreshing their gear
there versus building out

00:48:55.050 --> 00:48:57.860
the cloud using our services to
do the life and shift for them?

00:48:57.860 --> 00:48:59.850
They were actually saving
money by bringing us

00:48:59.850 --> 00:49:01.433
in to help them do
the lift and shift,

00:49:01.433 --> 00:49:04.940
and also paying for
the Google services.

00:49:04.940 --> 00:49:06.440
Thank you.

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:09.440
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:10.925 --> 00:49:11.800
CRAIG BOX: Thank you.

00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:14.630
All right, thank
you, Lee and Bob.

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:18.710
So in conclusion, very
quickly, three distinct changes

00:49:18.710 --> 00:49:20.070
we see here.

00:49:20.070 --> 00:49:22.550
Package your applications
and containers.

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:24.650
Make them work in clusters.

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:28.320
And then deploy
them to the cloud.

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:30.130
Here's a tweet I saw
a couple of days ago,

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:32.020
which really sums
this process up.

00:49:32.020 --> 00:49:36.090
So one guy, six months to move
from an RPM-based deployment

00:49:36.090 --> 00:49:39.960
platform using Docker
CI, Kubernetes,

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.760
doing continuous
deploy to Kubernetes.

00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:45.810
And took the deployment
process for the VPN software

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:48.160
from 8 hours to three minutes.

00:49:48.160 --> 00:49:49.650
Thank you very much.

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:52.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

